/-!
# Full kernels and the common fixer of the compatible Sylow pair

The actual maximum small mover acts abelianly on the full neighboring seed:
quadraticity gives this on the moved layer, and the incident central attachment
is fixed. It therefore preserves the common fixer of both critical kernels.
The simultaneous generation identities of the selected pair make the fixer
joined with the starting seed normal in the whole starting stabilizer.
The full-seed index bound eight then puts that fixer in the Baumann slice.
A strict slice consequently forces joint kernel movement larger than two.

For a trapped selected seed, failure of endpoint normalization excludes the
permutation extension of the full omega layer. Hence the whole endpoint X
is generated by its moved layer and its full central summand. This discharges
central generation before identifying the full X-kernel commutator with the
full seed-kernel commutator; the last-edge attachment is retained separately.
The full central summand lies in the incident edge omega, so central generation
also proves X = Z. Equal neighboring movers now have central omega exactly the
full starting-seed fixer. Their neighbor-seed commutators lie in that omega;
for the selected pair, the intersection of the two same-X-kernel commutators
is fixed by the whole starting stabilizer. No individual line equality follows
merely from this intersection estimate.

The application uses the same compatible edge, residual transporter, and
both transported critical paths in the equal-mover branch of MS93 (5.6).
It retains the literal original radical embedding and both cover orders.
The individual kernel-line bound, its conjugate comparison, residual transport
and the same-commutator alignment of (5.8) remain necessary, as do the unequal
mover, other-image and distance-four branches. No distance exclusion is asserted.

Source: Meierfrankenfeld--Stellmacher, Pushing up weak BN-pairs of rank two
(1993), (3.3)(ii) and (5.6)--(5.8), printed pp.866--867,917--920.
-/

